@Ms. Billie M. Spaight

It’s in the Bible. From Google:

"Jot and tittle" are the absolute smallest parts of written language, famous for appearing together in the biblical phrase "not one jot or tittle shall pass from the law" (Matthew 5:18). A jot is the smallest letter (the tiny Hebrew letter yod or Greek iota), while a tittle is an even smaller accent mark, dot, or pen stroke used to tell similar letters apart.

@Matt

Hebrew has נִקּוּד, or niqqud, the system of points and other small marks that indicate vowels. But they’re not called anything like tittles. And they’re usually omitted in ordinary writing, anyway. 

